      "completion_tokens": 1199,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance that is essential for life. It is a colorless, odorless, and tasteless liquid at standard temperature and pressure.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, from a chemical and physical perspective:\n\n### 1. Chemical Composition\nWater is a chemical compound with the formula **$H_2O$**. This means that every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\n\nThese atoms are chemically bonded together. The oxygen atom is slightly heavier and more \"electronegative\" (it pulls electrons toward itself) than the hydrogen atoms. This creates a slight imbalance in charge, making water a **polar molecule**. This polarity is what gives water its unique ability to dissolve other substances and stick to itself.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance found naturally on Earth that can exist in all three physical states simultaneously:\n*   **Solid:** As ice (frozen water).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we drink and bathe in.\n*   **Gas:** As water vapor (invisible steam).\n\n### 3. Key Properties\nWater has several physical properties that make it unlike almost any other liquid:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is the main transport mechanism in the human body and in nature (moving nutrients and waste).\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules like to stick to each other very strongly. This creates a \"skin\" on the surface of water, which allows small insects to walk across ponds without sinking.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This is why the ocean moderates the Earth's climate, keeping coastal areas cooler in the summer and warmer in the winter.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It makes up about **71% of the Earth's surface** and is the primary component of all known forms of life (humans are about 60% water by weight)."
